# Supabase Rules

## Client Usage — Which Client to Use

| Context | Client | Import |
|---------|--------|--------|
| Server Component / API Route | `createServerClient()` | `lib/supabase/server.ts` |
| Client Component | `createBrowserClient()` | `lib/supabase/client.ts` |

**Never use `service_role` key on the client side — ever.**

## Helper Pattern (lib/supabase/)
All DB queries go in `lib/supabase/` — never inline Supabase calls in components or API routes.

```typescript
// lib/supabase/subjects.ts
export async function getSubjectWithClasses(subjectId: string, userId: string) {
  const supabase = createServerClient()
  const { data, error } = await supabase
    .from("subjects")
    .select("*, classes(*)")
    .eq("id", subjectId)
    .eq("user_id", userId)  // belt-and-suspenders on top of RLS
    .order("order_index", { foreignTable: "classes" })
    .single()

  if (error) throw error
  return data
}
```

## RLS Reminder
RLS policies ensure users only see their own data. But still:
- Always `.eq("user_id", userId)` as an explicit filter — defense in depth
- When writing new queries, ask: "would this return another user's data if RLS were off?"

## Auth Pattern in Server Components
```typescript
import { createServerClient } from "@/lib/supabase/server"
import { redirect } from "next/navigation"

const supabase = createServerClient()
const { data: { user } } = await supabase.auth.getUser()
if (!user) redirect("/login")
```

## Error Handling
```typescript
const { data, error } = await supabase.from("subjects").select("*")
if (error) throw new Error(`Failed to fetch subjects: ${error.message}`)
// Never silently ignore errors
```

## Schema Reference
```sql
profiles    (id uuid PK, email text, full_name text, created_at)
subjects    (id uuid PK, user_id FK, name text, slug text, created_at)
classes     (id uuid PK, subject_id FK, order_index int, title text,
             topics jsonb, homework_markdown text, duration_minutes int=80, created_at)
```

## What to Avoid
- Supabase calls directly in `page.tsx` or component files
- Forgetting to handle `error` from Supabase responses
- Using `.single()` without handling the case where no row is found (use `notFound()`)
